1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,049
Participants inevitably move during an
fMRI session.

2
00:00:03,049 --> 00:00:09,148
Because voxels are small, a shift of just
one millimeter moves brain tissue across

3
00:00:09,148 --> 00:00:11,761
voxel boundaries, introducing massive
non-neural variance.

4
00:00:11,761 --> 00:00:17,424
Motion correction with MCFLIRT aligns each
acquired volume back to a common reference

5
00:00:17,424 --> 00:00:17,860
position.

6
00:00:22,267 --> 00:00:26,949
MCFLIRT uses a rigid-body transformation
with six degrees of freedom: three spatial

7
00:00:26,949 --> 00:00:32,803
translations along the x, y, and z axes,
and three rotations: pitch, roll, and yaw.

8
00:00:32,803 --> 00:00:38,266
Because the brain does not stretch or
shear within a scan, higher degrees of

9
00:00:38,266 --> 00:00:39,827
freedom would be unphysical.

10
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:47,416
MCFLIRT outputs two critical displacement
plots.

11
00:00:47,416 --> 00:00:52,946
Absolute displacement measures total drift
from the reference volume across the

12
00:00:52,946 --> 00:00:53,951
entire run.

13
00:00:53,951 --> 00:00:57,470
Relative displacement, or framewise
displacement, measures abrupt

14
00:00:57,470 --> 00:00:58,475
volume-to-volume jerks.

15
00:00:58,475 --> 00:01:04,005
Sudden spikes above half a voxel indicate
potential spin-history artifacts requiring

16
00:01:04,005 --> 00:01:05,010
volume censoring.

17
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:14,484
Always inspect both absolute and relative
motion plots before statistical modeling.

18
00:01:14,484 --> 00:01:20,021
Realign to the middle volume, flag sudden
jumps, and never apply spatial smoothing

19
00:01:20,021 --> 00:01:22,150
before motion estimation is complete.
